# Flutter Project Architecture

## 1. Why Do We Need Architecture?

When learning Flutter, you might initially put everything inside:

```text
lib/
└── main.dart
```

That's fine for small practice apps.

But a real app can contain:

```text
20+ screens
API calls
Models
Images
State management
Local database
Authentication
Reusable widgets
```

Putting everything into `main.dart` becomes difficult to maintain.

So we divide the code into folders.

---

# 2. Basic Structure

A good beginner-friendly structure is:

```text
lib/
│
├── main.dart
│
├── screens/
│   ├── home_screen.dart
│   ├── login_screen.dart
│   └── profile_screen.dart
│
├── widgets/
│   ├── custom_button.dart
│   └── custom_textfield.dart
│
├── models/
│   └── user.dart
│
├── services/
│   └── api_service.dart
│
└── providers/
    └── user_provider.dart
```

Each folder has a purpose.

---

# 3. `main.dart`

`main.dart` is usually the **starting point** of the application.

Example:

```dart
void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}
```

It can contain your top-level:

```text
MaterialApp
Theme
Routes
Providers
App configuration
```

Avoid putting the entire application inside it.

---

# 4. `screens/`

Put complete screens/pages here.

Examples:

```text
screens/
├── login_screen.dart
├── home_screen.dart
├── profile_screen.dart
├── settings_screen.dart
└── details_screen.dart
```

Example:

```dart
class HomeScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  const HomeScreen({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text("Home"),
      ),
      body: Center(
        child: Text("Home"),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

A screen usually represents something the user sees as a page.

---

# 5. `widgets/`

Put **reusable UI components** here.

For example, if many screens use the same button:

```text
widgets/
└── custom_button.dart
```

Example:

```dart
class CustomButton extends StatelessWidget {

  final String text;
  final VoidCallback onPressed;

  const CustomButton({
    super.key,
    required this.text,
    required this.onPressed,
  });

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return SizedBox(
      width: double.infinity,
      child: ElevatedButton(
        onPressed: onPressed,
        child: Text(text),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

Then use it:

```dart
CustomButton(
  text: "Login",
  onPressed: () {},
)
```

Instead of rewriting the same button on every screen.

---

# 6. `models/`

Models represent your **data**.

You already learned this with JSON.

Example:

```text
models/
└── user.dart
```

```dart
class User {

  final int id;
  final String name;
  final String email;

  User({
    required this.id,
    required this.name,
    required this.email,
  });

  factory User.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
    return User(
      id: json["id"],
      name: json["name"],
      email: json["email"],
    );
  }
}
```

So:

```text
JSON
 ↓
User Model
 ↓
UI
```

---

# 7. `services/`

Services handle things such as:

* API calls
* Database operations
* Authentication
* Other external operations

Example:

```text
services/
└── api_service.dart
```

Instead of putting API code directly inside your screen.

Example:

```dart
class ApiService {

  Future<void> getUsers() async {
    // API code
  }

}
```

Then the screen can call:

```dart
ApiService().getUsers();
```

The screen doesn't need to contain all the API logic.

---

# 8. `providers/`

If you're using Provider for state management:

```text
providers/
├── user_provider.dart
├── cart_provider.dart
└── theme_provider.dart
```

Example:

```dart
class CartProvider extends ChangeNotifier {

  List<String> cart = [];

  void addToCart(String item) {
    cart.add(item);
    notifyListeners();
  }

}
```

This keeps shared state separate from UI code.

---

# 9. How Everything Connects

A typical application can look like:

```text
                Flutter App
                    │
                    ↓
                 Screen
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          ↓                   ↓
       Provider            Service
          ↓                   ↓
       State                 API
                              ↓
                           JSON
                              ↓
                           Model
                              ↓
                            Screen
```

For example:

```text
HomeScreen
    ↓
UserProvider
    ↓
ApiService
    ↓
API
    ↓
JSON
    ↓
User Model
    ↓
Provider
    ↓
HomeScreen
```

---

# 10. Example Real Project

Suppose you're building a **Music Player**.

You could organize it like:

```text
lib/
│
├── main.dart
│
├── screens/
│   ├── home_screen.dart
│   ├── player_screen.dart
│   └── playlist_screen.dart
│
├── widgets/
│   ├── song_tile.dart
│   ├── search_bar.dart
│   └── player_controls.dart
│
├── models/
│   └── song.dart
│
├── services/
│   └── music_service.dart
│
└── providers/
    └── music_provider.dart
```

Now each part has a clear responsibility.

---

# 11. Don't Over-Organize Small Apps

You don't need 30 folders for a simple practice project.

For a small app:

```text
lib/
├── main.dart
├── home_screen.dart
└── second_screen.dart
```

is completely fine.

As the project grows, start separating things.

---

# 12. Important Principle — Separation of Responsibility

Try to follow:

```text
Screen
→ UI

Widget
→ Reusable UI

Model
→ Data structure

Service
→ API / database operations

Provider
→ Shared state
```

Don't mix everything together.

For example, avoid:

```text
Screen
 ├── UI
 ├── API code
 ├── database code
 ├── JSON parsing
 ├── state management
 └── 1000 lines of logic
```

Instead:

```text
Screen
 ↓
Provider
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Model
```

---

# 13. Easy Memory

```text
main.dart
    ↓
App starts here

screens/
    ↓
Complete pages

widgets/
    ↓
Reusable UI

models/
    ↓
Data structure

services/
    ↓
API / database

providers/
    ↓
Shared state
```

### Most important rule

**Don't organize your project based only on how many files you have. Organize it based on what each file is responsible for.**
